<Base
  title="Prepping"
  description="The boring kind of prepping — redundancy, resilience, and open-source tools that don't fail when systems do."
>
  <h1>Prepping</h1>
  <p>
    Not the bunker prepper. Not the wear-cargo-pants-to-the-supermarket
    prepper. The prepper who reads the insurance policy, runs the what-if,
    and builds redundancy because single points of failure are how grown
    adults end up suddenly broke at 45 or stuck without water in 38-degree
    heat. Sensible. Boring. Effective.
  </p>
  <p>
    The whole frame: build pillars that operate independently and compound on
    each other, lean on tools you actually own, and treat a catastrophe plan
    like an insurance premium — only useful if the work was done before you
    needed it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the leverage actually is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Power</strong> — solar with battery once the unit allows it. Until then, the mental models for graceful degradation when the grid hiccups.</li>
    <li><strong>Water</strong> — rainwater capture, simple filtration, knowing the local hazard maps before you need them.</li>
    <li><strong>Food</strong> — a balcony garden in development, a two-week pantry buffer, knowledge of which weeds growing in your suburb are edible.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills</strong> — basic electronics, DIY, knife sharpening, first aid. The library of <em>things you can do yourself</em> is the cheapest insurance there is.</li>
    <li><strong>Financial</strong> — debt down, second income, cash buffer, important documents backed up encrypted in two places.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital</strong> — open-source software where the platform can't pull the rug; offline-first where possible; local LLMs for the cases you don't want to pipe to a cloud.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Open-source tools I use or am learning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>QGIS</strong> — professional GIS for mapping local bushfire and flood hazard overlays. Every Australian state publishes the data; almost nobody uses it. Useful in construction too.</li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> — for resilience against vendor lock-in and as a low-cost OS on older hardware that would otherwise go to landfill.</li>
    <li><strong>Local LLMs on a Raspberry Pi</strong> — the assistant without the cloud. Slow but sovereign.</li>
    <li><strong>Archive.org's survival library</strong> — the books that matter when the internet doesn't.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Notes on resilience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/notes/resilience-requires-redundancy">Resilience requires redundancy, not single points of control</a></li>
    <li><a href="/notes/self-sufficiency-compounds">Self-sufficiency compounds — each pillar strengthens the others</a></li>
    <li><a href="/notes/catastrophe-planning">Catastrophe planning must happen before the catastrophe</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this isn't</h2>
  <p>
    It isn't doom-prep. It isn't anxiety about EMPs or society collapsing
    next Tuesday. The risks worth preparing for are the boring frequent ones:
    a power outage, a payment-system failure, an income disruption, a
    grinding rise in the price of imported goods, a heat event, a flood. If
    you're prepared for the boring catastrophes, you've covered most of the
    cinematic ones too.
  </p>
</Base>
